      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIMS",
          "content": "The association between SNPs of the human OPRM1 gene encoding the micro-opioid receptor and postoperative analgesic requirements in surgical patients remains controversial. Here, we evaluate whether any of the five tag SNPs (A118G, IVS2+G691C, IVS3+G5953A, IVS3+A8449G and TAA+A2109G) representing the four linkage disequilibrium blocks of the OPRM1 gene influences postoperative analgesic requirements."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS & METHODS",
          "content": "We studied 138 adult Japanese patients who underwent major open abdominal surgery under combined general and epidural anesthesia and received continuous postoperative epidural analgesia with opioids."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The 118G homozygous (GG) patients required 24-h postoperative analgesics more than 118A homozygous (AA) and heterozygous (AG) patients. Tag SNP haplotypes also were associated with 24-h postoperative analgesic requirements."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "These results suggest that OPRM1 gene tag SNP genotypes and haplotypes can primarily contribute to prediction of postoperative analgesic requirements in individual patients undergoing major open abdominal surgery."
        }
      ]},
